extra_fields.last_user_message_preview:
    Generate an LLM judge name and description based on the following form fields: {
  "instructions_prompt": "# Instructions\n\nAnalyze the user's question and classify it as either \"technical\" or \"non_technical\".\n\n## Classification Rules\n\n- Technical:\n  Questions related to programming, software development, APIs, databases, cloud computing, AI/ML, system design, networking, cybersecurity, DevOps, operating systems, technical troubleshooting, or other engineering concepts.\n\n- Non-technical:\n  Questions related to general knowledge, personal advice, health, fitness, relationships, travel, entertainment, business discussions without technical implementation details, creative writing, or everyday topics.\n\n## Response Format\n\nReturn the result in JSON format:\n\n{\n  \"analysis\": \"Brief reason for the classification\",\n  \"answer\": \"technical\" | \"non_technical\"\n}",
  "model": "mistral-medium-2505",
  "output": {
    "type": "CLASSIFICATION",
    "options": [
      {
        "value": "Technical ",
        "description": "The response is technical."
      },
      {
        "value": "non -technical",
        "description": "The response is non- technical."
      }
    ]
  },
  "tools": []
}

    It should reflect the judge role and intent, write an informative meaningful response that is perfect for a page title and description.

    Answer in the same language as the form fields. Also, be short - no more than 5 words for the name and 1 sentence for the description.

    Examples for name:
    - "Code quality score"
    - "Prime minister accuracy"
    - "Customer service response time"

    Examples for description:
    - "Evaluates the quality of the code generated by the LLM."
    - "Evaluates the accuracy of the prime minister's responses."
    - "Evaluates the response time of the customer service agent."
